   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Before or after the noun",
     "texto": [
      "The normal place of a Spanish adjective is **after** the noun, and there it **distinguishes**: **un coche rojo** is red and not another colour.\n\n**Before** the noun, an adjective does not distinguish. It **comments**: it adds an evaluation or something already known. That is why it sounds literary or emphatic: **un grave problema**, **la blanca nieve**, **una magnífica idea**.\n\nSome adjectives must go after: those of nationality, colour, shape and type. You say **un producto lácteo**, **una chica francesa**, never the other way round.\n\nA small group has two meanings. Before the noun the meaning is subjective, after it literal."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Two positions, two meanings",
     "texto": [
      "- un **viejo** amigo (of many years) / un amigo **viejo** (elderly)\n- un **pobre** hombre (unfortunate) / un hombre **pobre** (with no money)\n- un **gran** hombre (a great man) / un hombre **grande** (a big man)\n- mi **nuevo** coche (my latest) / mi coche **nuevo** (brand new)\n- mi **antiguo** piso (my former) / un piso **antiguo** (an old flat)\n- mi **única** hija (my only) / una hija **única** (an only child)\n- **cierta** información (some) / información **cierta** (true)\n- un **simple** camarero (a mere waiter) / un camarero **simple** (simple-minded)\n- el **mismo** director (the same) / el director **mismo** (himself)\n\n**Grande** becomes gran before a singular noun: un gran día. Approximate colours take a suffix and go after: rojo → **rojizo**, amarillo → **amarillento**, azul → **azulado**, verde → **verdoso**.\n\nAn adjective can also describe the subject through the verb: Ana llegó cansada. It agrees with the subject."
     ],
